// ShirtPanels — the shirt's PRINTABLE PANELS, read straight off the mesh (Pass 11).
//
// Pass 10 knew about one surface: the front chest. This is the generalisation — the
// whole garment, panel by panel, with everything a sticker needs to live on any of
// them. It supersedes Pass 10's ShirtUVGrid.ts, whose single front-panel grid is now
// PANELS[PANEL_FRONT].
//
// =============================================================================
// THE ATLAS SURVEY (Pass-11 point 0) — tshirt_tshirt_0.mesh, 1634 verts, 2640 tris
// =============================================================================
// UV islands were found as connected components of the triangle graph under shared
// VERTEX INDEX: the importer duplicates vertices along every UV seam, so vertex
// connectivity IS island connectivity. Sixteen islands, covering 59.8% of the atlas
// square, and — checked by rasterising every island onto a 512x512 grid — NO TWO
// ISLANDS OVERLAP anywhere in UV. Every panel below therefore owns its region of the
// bake outright, which is what makes a per-panel stamp safe.
//
//   #    tris  u range          v range          avg normal            role
//   0    1036  0.002 - 0.590    0.324 - 0.993    ( 0.00, 0.29,  0.96)  FRONT torso
//   1     688  0.573 - 0.967    0.345 - 0.963    ( 0.00, 0.19, -0.98)  BACK torso
//   2     210  0.710 - 0.939    0.160 - 0.356    ( 0.06, 0.09, -0.99)  right sleeve, back half
//   3     210  0.357 - 0.548    0.105 - 0.385    (-0.06, 0.09, -0.99)  left sleeve, back half
//   4     140  0.193 - 0.350    0.135 - 0.325    ( 0.43, 0.21,  0.88)  right sleeve, front half
//   5     140  0.745 - 0.925    0.028 - 0.215    (-0.43, 0.21,  0.88)  left sleeve, front half
//   6      44  0.961 - 0.997    0.371 - 0.998    ( 0.00, 0.27,  0.96)  front hem band
//   7      30  0.565 - 0.577    0.176 - 0.393    ( 0.28,-0.14, -0.95)  right cuff, back strip
//   8      30  0.956 - 0.969    0.154 - 0.371    (-0.28,-0.14, -0.95)  left cuff, back strip
//   9      20  0.913 - 0.942    0.080 - 0.230    (-0.39,-0.01,  0.92)  left cuff, front strip
//  10      20  0.751 - 0.899    0.007 - 0.050    ( 0.39,-0.01,  0.92)  right cuff, front strip
//  11      18  0.978 - 0.998    0.365 - 0.528    ( 0.24, 0.67,  0.71)  collar, front
//  12      16  0.002 - 0.016    0.277 - 0.393    ( 0.00, 0.64, -0.77)  collar, back
//  13      14  0.138 - 0.164    0.155 - 0.352    (-0.44, 0.19, -0.88)  back hem band, left
//  14      14  0.161 - 0.189    0.150 - 0.348    ( 0.44, 0.19, -0.88)  back hem band, right
//  15      10  0.057 - 0.068    0.303 - 0.390    (-0.50, 0.72,  0.49)  collar, side
//
// A BACK PANEL EXISTS: island #1, 688 triangles, disjoint from the front, and almost as
// large — 19.6% of the atlas against the front's 27.1%. The unwrap is remarkably
// uniform: every island measures about 47.5 cm per unit of UV to within a few percent,
// so a sticker keeps its size wherever it is put.
//
// Islands 6-15 are hem bands, cuff strips and collar pieces — a few tens of triangles
// each, and all under 0.01 of UV area. Nothing usefully sticker-sized fits on them, so
// the six PANELS below are the printable set: two torsos and four sleeve halves. (Each
// sleeve is unwrapped as TWO islands, a front half and a back half, split along the
// sleeve's own top seam.)
//
// =============================================================================
// WHAT EACH PANEL CARRIES
// =============================================================================
// THE CLAMP RECT — rectU/rectV, halfRightMesh/halfUpMesh.
//   The rectangle a sticker may occupy, given as a centre in UV plus half-extents in
//   MESH units along the panel's own (right, up) axes. It is the largest rectangle that
//   fits entirely INSIDE the island, found by the histogram method over a 400x400
//   rasterisation of the island — but rasterised IN THE PANEL'S OWN CLOTH FRAME, not in
//   UV. That distinction is what makes the sleeves usable at all: their unwraps are
//   turned 30 to 152 degrees in the atlas, so the biggest UV-axis-aligned rectangle
//   inside one is a small diamond-inscribed box, while the biggest rectangle along the
//   sleeve's OWN axes is most of the sleeve. Measured in centimetres on the cloth at the
//   shipped fit:
//
//     panel           usable rect      largest upright sticker it can hold
//     front           15.85 x 25.37    scale 1.83
//     back            11.34 x 25.86    scale 1.31
//     sleeve halves    4.4-4.9 x 5.6-6.1  scale 0.51-0.57
//
//   Working in the cloth frame also makes the runtime clamp simpler: a sticker is a
//   SQUARE in centimetres, so its footprint there is side x (|cos rot| + |sin rot|) on
//   both axes and the atlas rotation drops out entirely.
//
//   This replaces Pass 10's FRONT_PANEL_ISLAND, which was a bounding box of a SUBSET of
//   the front island and so contained 11.2% HOLES — armpit notches and the neckline —
//   that a sticker's footprint could reach and be clipped by. An inscribed rectangle
//   cannot do that: if the footprint is inside the rect it is on the cloth, which is
//   what "never half-drawn" requires. Verified by sampling: 2500 of 2500 random points
//   inside every panel's rect land on real triangles.
//
// homeU/homeV — where a newly placed sticker lands. For the two torsos this is the
//   island's horizontal centre at HOME_V_FRAC of its height, which for the front
//   evaluates to (0.296, 0.700) — exactly Pass 10's INITIAL_STICKER_UV, so the front's
//   behaviour is unchanged. For the sleeves it is the clamp rect's centre.
//
// meshPerU/meshPerV — mesh units per unit of u / v, the local derivative at the rect
//   centre. StickerSystem multiplies these (and the half-extents above) by the shirt's
//   live cm-per-mesh-unit, so changing SHIRT_TARGET_HEIGHT_CM still flows through
//   everything.
//
// atlasRotDeg — how far this island's unwrap is TURNED in the atlas, measured in the
//   panel's own (right, up) frame. Both torsos come out at 0.0 degrees: their u axis
//   runs to the viewer's left and their v axis runs up, the same convention on the back
//   as on the front, so a sticker stamped on the back reads the right way round and
//   turns the same way. The sleeves sit at 30 to 152 degrees, and the compositor folds
//   that straight into the stamp's own rotation.
//
// nx/ny/nz — the island's area-weighted outward normal in MESH space. This decides
//   which panel is facing the camera (placement) and which are live (the facing guard).
//
// pickWeight — how strongly this panel competes to be the one a new sticker lands on.
//   The torsos are the primary print surfaces at 1.0; the sleeves are secondary, so a
//   sleeve only wins when it is clearly the thing facing you and neither torso is.
//
// gridU0..gridV1, gnu/gnv, gridAt — the sampled mesh. Mesh-local (x, y, z) taken off the
//   mesh by barycentric lookup on a regular grid over the clamp rect's UV bounding box
//   (plus a little padding), bilinearly interpolated at runtime. The rect is rotated in
//   UV on the sleeves, so a handful of grid cells at the corners of that bounding box
//   fall OFF the island; those are filled from a cubic least-squares fit of the SAME
//   panel, so interpolation degrades into a smooth continuation of that panel rather
//   than snapping to a neighbour's geometry. Accuracy against the mesh, inside the clamp
//   rect where a sticker can actually be, in cm:
//
//     panel           max x/y/z            rms x/y/z
//     front           0.70 / 0.20 / 0.81   0.037 / 0.011 / 0.047
//     back            0.33 / 0.74 / 0.82   0.021 / 0.033 / 0.057
//     sleeves         0.17 / 0.14 / 0.12   0.017 / 0.013 / 0.024
//
//   (Pass 10's front grid was 0.52 / 0.12 / 0.68 max, 0.05 / 0.01 / 0.06 rms — so the
//   front is unchanged in practice, and the rest of the garment now matches it.)
//
// =============================================================================
// REGENERATING — THE SHIRT-SWAP POINT
// =============================================================================
// Swapping to a different garment means re-running the offline mesh reader and replacing
// THIS FILE plus the fit block in Stickers.ts. Nothing else changes. The reader:
//   1. parse the `.mesh` (stride-48 position/normal/tangent/texture0 vertex block,
//      UInt16 indices);
//   2. union-find the triangles by vertex index to get the UV islands;
//   3. drop islands too small to hold a sticker;
//   4. for each survivor take its area-weighted normal, build right = worldUp x normal
//      and up = normal x right, and measure meshPerU/meshPerV/atlasRotDeg from the local
//      derivative at its centre expressed in that frame;
//   5. find the largest inscribed rectangle IN THAT FRAME (not in UV);
//   6. sample the grid over the rectangle's UV bounding box, filling off-island cells
//      from a per-panel cubic fit.
// TAKE EVERY TRIANGLE in an island's UV bbox that is not turned to the BACK, not just
// the strongly outward-facing ones: on this shirt the 117 triangles where the torso
// wraps round the sides belong to the same UV island and are exactly the cloth a
// max-size sticker's corners land on.

/** Where a new sticker lands vertically inside a torso panel. Chosen so the front
 *  evaluates to Pass 10's INITIAL_STICKER_UV exactly. */
export const HOME_V_FRAC = 0.623

/** One printable surface of the garment. See the header for every field. */
export interface Panel {
  name: string
  // clamp rect: centre in UV, half-extents in MESH units along (right, up)
  rectU: number
  rectV: number
  halfRightMesh: number
  halfUpMesh: number
  // where a new sticker lands
  homeU: number
  homeV: number
  // atlas metrics
  meshPerU: number
  meshPerV: number
  atlasRotDeg: number
  // outward normal, mesh space
  nx: number
  ny: number
  nz: number
  pickWeight: number
  // sampled mesh: domain, resolution, and offset into PANEL_GRID
  gridU0: number
  gridU1: number
  gridV0: number
  gridV1: number
  gnu: number
  gnv: number
  gridAt: number
  /**
   * Pass 23 — which BAKE TARGET this panel prints into. A garment whose print surfaces
   * share one UV chart on separate materials (the socks: two socks, two materials, one
   * chart) gets one bake per group, and every panel says which. Absent means 0, which is
   * what every single-bake garment has always had.
   */
  target?: number
}

